{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' FormalConjectures/OEIS/63880.lean\nrg -n --hidden -S \\\"unitary|Unitary|87360|1052\\\" FormalConjecturesForMathlib FormalConjectures --glob '*.lean'\ngit ls-tree -r --name-only HEAD | rg '(\"'^|/)(1052|.*[Uu]nitary.*)('\"\\\\.|/|\"'$)|ErdosProblems/1052'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"/-\nCopyright 2026 The Formal Conjectures Authors.\n\nLicensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (the \"License\");\nyou may not use this file except in compliance with the License.\nYou may obtain a copy of the License at\n\n    https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\n\nUnless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing, software\ndistributed under the License is distributed on an \"AS IS\" BASIS,\nWITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied.\nSee the License for the specific language governing permissions and\nlimitations under the License.\n-/\nimport FormalConjecturesUtil\n\n/-!\n# Conjectures associated with A063880\n\nA063880 lists numbers $n$ such that $\\sigma(n) = 2 \\cdot \\text{usigma}(n)$, where $\\sigma(n)$ is the\nsum of all divisors and $\\text{usigma}(n)$ is the sum of unitary divisors.\n\nEquivalently, these are numbers whose unitary and non-unitary divisors have equal sum.\n\nThe conjectures state that all members satisfy $n \\equiv 108 \\pmod{216}$, and that all\nprimitive terms (those whose proper divisors aren't in the sequence) are powerful numbers,\nwith $108$ being the only primitive term.\n\n*References:*\n- [A063880](https://oeis.org/A063880)\n-/\n\nnamespace OeisA63880\n\nopen scoped ArithmeticFunction.sigma\n\n/-- The set of unitary divisors of $n$: divisors $d$ such that $\\gcd(d, n/d) = 1$. -/\ndef unitaryDivisors (n : ℕ) : Finset ℕ :=\n  {d ∈ n.divisors | d.Coprime (n / d)}\n\n/-- The sum of unitary divisors of $n$, denoted $\\text{usigma}(n)$. -/\ndef usigma (n : ℕ) : ℕ :=\n  ∑ d ∈ unitaryDivisors n, d\n\n/-- A number $n$ is in the sequence A063880 if $\\sigma(n) = 2 \\cdot \\text{usigma}(n)$. -/\ndef A (n : ℕ) : Prop :=\n  0 < n ∧ σ 1 n = 2 * usigma n\n\n/-- A term $n$ is primitive if no proper divisor of $n$ is in the sequence. -/\nabbrev IsPrimitiveTerm (n : ℕ) : Prop := {n | A n}.IsPrimitive n\n\n/-- $108$ is in the sequence A063880. -/\n@[category test, AMS 11]\ntheorem a_108 : A 108 := by\n  refine ⟨by norm_num, ?_⟩\n  decide\n\n/-- $540$ is in the sequence A063880. -/\n@[category test, AMS 11]\ntheorem a_540 : A 540 := by\n  refine ⟨by norm_num, ?_⟩\n  decide\n\n/-- $756$ is in the sequence A063880. -/\n@[category test, AMS 11]\ntheorem a_756 : A 756 := by\n  refine ⟨by norm_num, ?_⟩\n  decide\n\n/-- $108$ is a primitive term. -/\n@[category test, AMS 11]\ntheorem isPrimitiveTerm_108 : IsPrimitiveTerm 108 := by\n  rw [IsPrimitiveTerm, Set.isPrimitive_iff]\n  refine ⟨a_108, ?_⟩\n  intro d hd\n  have ⟨hdvd, hlt⟩ := Nat.mem_properDivisors.mp hd\n  interval_cases d <;> simp_all [A] <;> decide\n\n/-- All members of the sequence satisfy $n \\equiv 108 \\pmod{216}$. -/\n@[category research open, AMS 11]\ntheorem mod_216_of_a {n : ℕ} (h : A n) : n % 216 = 108 := by\n  sorry\n\n/-- All primitive terms are powerful numbers. -/\n@[category textbook, AMS 11]\ntheorem powerful_of_isPrimitiveTerm {n : ℕ} (h : IsPrimitiveTerm n) : n.Powerful := by\n  sorry\n\n/-- $108$ is the only primitive term. -/\n@[category research open, AMS 11]\ntheorem unique_primitive_108 {n : ℕ} (h : IsPrimitiveTerm n) : n = 108 := by\n  sorry\n\n/-- If $m$ is a primitive term and $s$ is squarefree with $\\gcd(m, s) = 1$, then $m \\cdot s$\nis in the sequence. -/\n@[category textbook, AMS 11]\ntheorem a_of_primitive_mul_squarefree (m s : ℕ) (hm : IsPrimitiveTerm m)\n    (hs : Squarefree s) (hcoprime : m.Coprime s) : A (m * s) := by\n  sorry\n\n/-- Non-primitive terms have the form $m \\cdot s$ where $m$ is primitive and $s$ is\nsquarefree with $\\gcd(m, s) = 1$. -/\n@[category research solved, AMS 11]\ntheorem exists_primitive_of_a {n : ℕ} (h : A n) :\n    ∃ m s, IsPrimitiveTerm m ∧ Squarefree s ∧ m.Coprime s ∧ n = m * s := by\n  sorry\n\nend OeisA63880\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/35.lean:38:  *Absolutely Maximally Entangled states, combinatorial designs and multi-unitary matrices*,\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:40:In this file, an orthonormal basis is represented by a unitary matrix whose columns are the\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:41:basis vectors. For two such bases `U` and `V`, the matrix `relativeUnitary U V`, which is\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:44:$\\| (relativeUnitary\\ U\\ V)_{ij} \\|^2 = 1 / d$\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:126:/-- A unitary matrix representing an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{C}^d$ via its columns. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:127:abbrev UMat (d : ℕ) := ↥(Matrix.unitaryGroup (Fin d) ℂ)\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:129:/-- The relative unitary between two bases. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:130:def relativeUnitary {d : ℕ} (U V : UMat d) : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℂ :=\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:133:/-- Two unitary matrices represent mutually unbiased bases if every entry of the relative\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:134:unitary has squared norm $1 / d$. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:136:  ∀ i j : Fin d, ‖relativeUnitary U V i j‖ ^ (2 : ℕ) = (d : ℝ)⁻¹\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:143:  have hstar : relativeUnitary V U = star (relativeUnitary U V) := by\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:144:    simp [relativeUnitary, Matrix.star_mul]\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:146:    ‖relativeUnitary V U i j‖ ^ (2 : ℕ)\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:147:        = ‖star (relativeUnitary U V) i j‖ ^ (2 : ℕ) := by simp [hstar]\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:148:    _ = ‖relativeUnitary U V j i‖ ^ (2 : ℕ) := by simp\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:151:/-- A family of unitary matrices is a family of mutually unbiased bases if every two distinct\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:232:/-- Scaling a phase matrix by $\\omega$ produces a unitary matrix whenever the phase has unit modulus. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:234:lemma scaled_phaseMatrix_mem_unitary {ζ : ℂ} (hζ : star ζ * ζ = 1) :\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:235:    (ω • phaseMatrix ζ) ∈ Matrix.unitaryGroup (Fin 2) ℂ := by\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:236:  rw [Matrix.mem_unitaryGroup_iff']\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:258:  ⟨ω • phaseMatrix ζ, scaled_phaseMatrix_mem_unitary hζ⟩\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:280:/-- The qubit $X$ basis as a bundled unitary matrix. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:283:/-- The qubit $Y$ basis as a bundled unitary matrix. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:292:  · simp [relativeUnitary, ZU, phaseU, phaseMatrix, omega_norm_sq]\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:293:  · simp [relativeUnitary, ZU, phaseU, phaseMatrix, omega_norm_sq]\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:294:  all_goals simpa [relativeUnitary, ZU, phaseU, phaseMatrix, norm_mul] using\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:297:/-- If $\\overline{\\zeta}\\,\\eta = i$, then the relative unitary between the corresponding phase\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:302:    relativeUnitary (phaseU ζ hζ) (phaseU η hη) = !![ω, star ω;\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:305:    relativeUnitary (phaseU ζ hζ) (phaseU η hη) =\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:308:      simpa [relativeUnitary, phaseU, sub_eq_add_neg] using\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:361:/-- The first entry of the first column of a qubit unitary basis matrix. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:366:/-- The second entry of the first column of a qubit unitary basis matrix. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:381:/-- The $(0,0)$ entry of the relative unitary is the overlap of the first columns. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:383:lemma relativeUnitary_apply_zero_zero (U V : UMat 2) :\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:384:    relativeUnitary U V 0 0 = star (u0 U) * u0 V + star (u1 U) * u1 V := by\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:385:  simp [relativeUnitary, u0, u1, Matrix.mul_apply, Fin.sum_univ_two]\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:387:/-- The first column of a unitary matrix has squared norm $1$. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:392:    exact (Matrix.mem_unitaryGroup_iff').mp U.2\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:405:/-- The Bloch inner product is determined by the $(0,0)$ entry of the relative unitary. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:408:    inner ℝ (bloch U) (bloch V) = 2 * Complex.normSq (relativeUnitary U V 0 0) - 1 := by\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:418:      Complex.normSq (relativeUnitary U V 0 0) =\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:421:    simpa [relativeUnitary_apply_zero_zero, Complex.normSq_mul, mul_assoc] using\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:448:/-- The relative unitary of a basis with itself is the identity matrix. -/\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:450:lemma relativeUnitary_self (U : UMat 2) : relativeUnitary U U = 1 := by\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:451:  rw [relativeUnitary]\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:452:  exact Matrix.UnitaryGroup.star_mul_self U\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:457:  rw [bloch_inner_eq_two_normSq_sub_one, relativeUnitary_self]\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:473:  have h00 : Complex.normSq (relativeUnitary U V 0 0) = (2 : ℝ)⁻¹ := by\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:475:      Complex.normSq (relativeUnitary U V 0 0) = ‖relativeUnitary U V 0 0‖ ^ 2 := by\nFormalConjectures/OpenQuantumProblems/13.lean:476:        simpa using (RCLike.normSq_eq_def' (relativeUnitary U V 0 0))\nFormalConjectures/OEIS/63880.lean:22:sum of all divisors and $\\text{usigma}(n)$ is the sum of unitary divisors.\nFormalConjectures/OEIS/63880.lean:24:Equivalently, these are numbers whose unitary and non-unitary divisors have equal sum.\nFormalConjectures/OEIS/63880.lean:38:/-- The set of unitary divisors of $n$: divisors $d$ such that $\\gcd(d, n/d) = 1$. -/\nFormalConjectures/OEIS/63880.lean:39:def unitaryDivisors (n : ℕ) : Finset ℕ :=\nFormalConjectures/OEIS/63880.lean:42:/-- The sum of unitary divisors of $n$, denoted $\\text{usigma}(n)$. -/\nFormalConjectures/OEIS/63880.lean:44:  ∑ d ∈ unitaryDivisors n, d\nFormalConjectures/Wikipedia/DeterminantalConjecture.lean:26:$U_2 D_2 U_2^*$ respectively, where $U_1, U_2$ are unitary and $D_1, D_2$\nFormalConjectures/Wikipedia/DeterminantalConjecture.lean:45:    (d1 d2 : n → ℂ) (U1 U2 : unitary (Matrix n n ℂ)) :\nFormalConjectures/Subsets/FC100SolvedSet1.lean:22:import FormalConjectures.ErdosProblems.«1052»\nFormalConjectures/Subsets/FC100SolvedSet1.lean:212:  decl_name% Erdos1052.isUnitaryPerfect_60,\nFormalConjectures/GreensOpenProblems/33.lean:25:  subsets for unitary representations.\" Confluentes Mathematici 12.1 (2020): 31-68.\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/40.lean:81:    simp only [one_mem, CStarRing.norm_of_mem_unitary, RCLike.norm_natCast, Nat.one_le_cast]\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:20:# Erdős Problem 1052\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:22:*Reference:* [erdosproblems.com/1052](https://www.erdosproblems.com/1052)\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:25:namespace Erdos1052\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:27:/-- A proper unitary divisor of $n$ is a divisor $d$ of $n$\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:29:def properUnitaryDivisors (n : ℕ) : Finset ℕ :=\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:32:/-- A number $n > 0$ is a unitary perfect number if it is the sum of its proper unitary divisors. -/\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:33:def IsUnitaryPerfect (n : ℕ) : Prop :=\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:34:  ∑ i ∈ properUnitaryDivisors n, i = n ∧ 0 < n\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:37:Are there only finitely many unitary perfect numbers? -/\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:39:theorem erdos_1052 :\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:40:    answer(sorry) ↔ {n | IsUnitaryPerfect n}.Finite := by\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:44:All unitary perfect numbers are even.\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:48:@[category research solved, AMS 11, formal_proof using formal_conjectures at \"https://github.com/mzhorvath1/formal-conjectures/blob/b70a2ddf5e55f743aac9d4f4a907786b39bc9807/FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean#L46\"]\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:49:theorem even_of_isUnitaryPerfect (n : ℕ) (hn : IsUnitaryPerfect n) : Even n := by\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:53:theorem isUnitaryPerfect_6 : IsUnitaryPerfect 6 := by\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:54:  norm_num [IsUnitaryPerfect, properUnitaryDivisors]\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:58:theorem isUnitaryPerfect_60 : IsUnitaryPerfect 60 := by\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:59:  norm_num [IsUnitaryPerfect, properUnitaryDivisors]\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:63:theorem isUnitaryPerfect_90 : IsUnitaryPerfect 90 := by\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:64:  norm_num [IsUnitaryPerfect, properUnitaryDivisors]\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:68:theorem isUnitaryPerfect_87360 : IsUnitaryPerfect 87360 := by\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:71:  norm_num [IsUnitaryPerfect, properUnitaryDivisors]\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:74:@[category test, AMS 11, formal_proof using formal_conjectures at \"https://github.com/Sanexxxx777/formal-conjectures/blob/be8aed15e8888a08bbe723170698e26c046412a4/FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean#L346\"]\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:75:theorem isUnitaryPerfect_146361946186458562560000 : IsUnitaryPerfect 146361946186458562560000 := by\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean:78:end Erdos1052\nFormalConjectures/OEIS/105210.lean:20:# Conjectures associated with A105210\nFormalConjectures/OEIS/105210.lean:26:- [A105210](https://oeis.org/A105210)\nFormalConjectures/OEIS/105210.lean:29:namespace OeisA105210\nFormalConjectures/OEIS/105210.lean:69:  This is the general form of the sequence A105210 for a starting value $k$. -/\nFormalConjectures/OEIS/105210.lean:107:end OeisA105210\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1052.lean\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
